{"conjecture": "In any software dependency graph, once the number of cross-module links exceeds a critical density proportional to the square root of the module count, non-contractible dependency loops inevitably form. These loops represent topological defects that make the system resistant to local refactoring: any single module replacement leaves the global loop intact, merely shifting the constraint to a neighboring module. Only a simultaneous, coordinated replacement of the entire loop can untangle it, explaining why no sequence of individually correct, locally reviewed changes can ever reverse the entanglement."}
